DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The pptthhrreeaadd__rrwwlloocckkaattttrr__sseettkkiinndd__nnpp() function sets the "lock  kind"  at‐
       tribute  of the read‐write lock attribute object referred to by _a_t_t_r to
       the value specified in _p_r_e_f.  The argument _p_r_e_f may be set  to  one  of
       the following:

       PPTTHHRREEAADD__RRWWLLOOCCKK__PPRREEFFEERR__RREEAADDEERR__NNPP
              This  is  the  default.   A thread may hold multiple read locks;
              that is, read locks are recursive.  According to The Single Unix
              Specification, the behavior is unspecified when a  reader  tries
              to  place  a  lock,  and  there is no write lock but writers are
              waiting.   Giving  preference  to  the  reader,  as  is  set  by
              PPTTHHRREEAADD__RRWWLLOOCCKK__PPRREEFFEERR__RREEAADDEERR__NNPP,  implies  that  the reader will
              receive the requested lock, even if a  writer  is  waiting.   As
              long as there are readers, the writer will be starved.

       PPTTHHRREEAADD__RRWWLLOOCCKK__PPRREEFFEERR__WWRRIITTEERR__NNPP
              This is intended as the write lock analog of PPTTHHRREEAADD__RRWWLLOOCCKK__PPRREE‐‐
              FFEERR__RREEAADDEERR__NNPP.   This  is ignored by glibc because the POSIX re‐
              quirement to support recursive read locks would cause  this  op‐
              tion     to    create    trivial    deadlocks;    instead    use
              PPTTHHRREEAADD__RRWWLLOOCCKK__PPRREEFFEERR__WWRRIITTEERR__NNOONNRREECCUURRSSIIVVEE__NNPP which  ensures  the
              application  developer  will  not take recursive read locks thus
              avoiding deadlocks.

       PPTTHHRREEAADD__RRWWLLOOCCKK__PPRREEFFEERR__WWRRIITTEERR__NNOONNRREECCUURRSSIIVVEE__NNPP
              Setting the lock kind to this avoids writer starvation  as  long
              as any read locking is not done in a recursive fashion.

       The  pptthhrreeaadd__rrwwlloocckkaattttrr__ggeettkkiinndd__nnpp()  function returns the value of the
       lock kind attribute of the read‐write lock attribute object referred to
       by _a_t_t_r in the pointer _p_r_e_f.

